tic primary turbulence in the state (the governor's upset loss) hints at brand strain but has limited bearing on the presidential general"], "rationale": "With no polls or expert ratings available, the forecast rests on Rhode Island's roughly eight-point Democratic lean, which favors the Democratic nominee in an open-seat contest. The state's Democratic primary upheaval is real but reflects an unpopular incumbent rather than evidence about the presidential matchup.", "watch": "The national political environment and the identity of each party's eventual nominee could move Rhode Island's margin substantially in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island remains a Democratic-leaning state in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of about eight points toward Democrats. The 2028 race is open because the incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party benefits from incumbency. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so fundamentals carry the forecast and uncertainty is wide.\n\nRecent headlines show some Democratic turbulence in the state: Governor McKee lost a primary to Helena Foulkes amid frustration over the Washington Bridge, and Gina Raimondo stepped back from a 2028 run. Those developments shape the Democratic primary picture more than the general election. The state's underlying lean points to a Democratic win, though this far out the race could shift with the national environment and the nominees.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8249, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7734, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7988, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7688, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.81